          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{\u2032}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{\u2032}+y\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{\u2032}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x={y}^{6}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${y}^{\u2032}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$ and ${y}^{\u2032}+y\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ only."
