          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-108920",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nI. ${y}^{1/3}{y}^{\u2032}+\\sqrt{x}{y}^{1/3}={y}^{3}$\n\nII. ${y}^{\u2032}-y\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y$\n\nIII. $y\\,{y}^{\u2032}+\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}=1$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+\\sqrt{x}={y}^{8/3}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""
        },
