          ":question/id": "e0290e80-a1be-52e2-9a1f-466e9d58d7c6",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-108912",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nI. ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n\nII. ${y}^{\u2032}-y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$\n\nIII. ${y}^{\u2032}+y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x}{{y}^{2}}$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3},\\,q(x)=-1,\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x,\\,n=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""
        }
      ],
