              ":question/id": "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-8965",
              ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"
            },
